.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1209 AM PDT Wed Aug 26 2026
(Today and tonight)

Stratus clouds are developing at the immediate coast and will expand
into the coastal valleys through the night, before mixing out
Wednesday morning. The weather pattern today remains dominated by
the balance between ridging in the Desert Southwest and troughing in
the eastern Pacific, with a slight edge to the ridging as reflected
in a general warming trend across the region. Highs in the inland
valleys reach the middle 80s to the middle 90s, up to 106 within the
warmest regions of southern Monterey County, with the Bays reaching
the middle 70s to the middle 80s and the Pacific coast remaining
rather cool in the lower to middle 60s. A gentle to moderate onshore
breeze develops in the afternoon and evening hours with gusts of 20
to 30 mph at the coast, through gaps and passes, and within the
Salinas Valley. The marine layer will remain around 1000-1500 ft
today, with warm and dry conditions above the marine layer
inversion. See the FIRE WEATHER section for the implications for
local fire weather concerns.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1209 AM PDT Wed Aug 26 2026
(Thursday through Tuesday)

The ridging remains in place through Thursday with temperature
around the same as today`s before the trough over the eastern
Pacific begins to migrate eastwards late Thursday into Friday,
starting a cooling trend that will last through the early part of
next week. As the trough moves into the West Coast, it will pull in
tropical moisture partially from what would be the remnants of
Tropical Storm Iselle and direct it towards the state. The model
trend for steadily increasing Precipitable Water values has
continued to play out, with ensemble model probabilities suggesting
that Oakland is likely to see precipitable water values exceeding
1.0 inches on Friday morning. For context, the 90th percentile value
for precipitable water at OAK is 1.04 inches, and pretty much all
the deterministic models that go out this far have OAK exceeding
this value, with some of the wetter models making a run for or
exceeding the daily maximum value, which for the 12Z (5 AM PDT)
soundings on August 28, would be 1.24 inches. This kind of setup
would definitely bring high clouds to the region and also brings the
risk of high-based rain showers and thunderstorms to the region. One
of the big caveats of this risk, especially as it pertains to
thunderstorms, is that model output also suggests that instability
is lacking with the European model showing virtually no CAPE
anywhere within the atmosphere, surface or elevated. We will
continue to monitor the evolution of this setup closely.

The incoming trough will also lead to an tightening of the pressure
gradient, which in turn will lead to breezier winds Friday into the
weekend. Gusts will rise to 25 to 35 mph along the coast and through
favored gaps, valleys, and passes. The troughing pattern will
continue to develop into the early part of next week, when
temperatures will dip 5 to 15 degrees below the seasonal average,
with highs in the 70s to lower 80s in the interior valleys. The
current forecast has some rain chances returning towards the
middle of next week, but this is critically dependent on the
future evolution of the situation in the tropical East Pacific,
and as a result the confidence in this forecast is very low at
this time.

