.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 1209 AM PDT Wed Aug 26 2026

Not much has changed in terms of the fire weather threat, with near
critical fire weather conditions persisting in the higher elevations
above 1000 feet through Thursday due to warm and dry conditions.
Overnight humidity recoveries remain poor to very poor within these
areas and the impact of the marine layer inversion leads to
temperatures warmer than the seasonal average, especially within the
Santa Lucia range and the interior Central Coast where temperature
are running as much as 15 to 20 degrees above the seasonal
average. Model output suggests that an offshore flow has set up
over the Big Sur coast at the 925 mb level, supporting the risk
for overnight nocturnal drying in the interior Central Coast and
particularly surrounding the Timber Fire. Last night I had noted
that the southwest winds seemed to bring in more moist air into
the Santa Lucia range, and that the overnight diminishing of winds
when combined with the offshore push led to an unusual situation
where the maximum daily relative humidities took place during the
day. This remains the case, up until the ridgeline parallel to the
Big Sur coast when the southwest winds begin to downslope and dry
the region. Unfortunately there aren`t a lot of weather station
in these areas to verify the conceptual model. Smoke transport
from the Timber Fire will continue to peak in the afternoon and
evening with the plume spreading towards the Salinas area. Friday
into the weekend, a pattern change should allow for some relief as
an upper level trough comes into the region bringing cooler
temperatures and a deeper marine layer.

